## The Heart of Iteration

```xiom
pub interface Iterator {
  type Item;
  fn next(self: &mut Self) -> Option[Self::Item];
  fn size_hint(self: &Self) -> (Int, Option[Int]) {
    return (0, None());  // Default: unknown size
  }
}
```

### How next() Works
```xiom
let mut iter = vec.iter();
// iter.next() returns Some(first_element)
// iter.next() returns Some(second_element)
// ...
// iter.next() returns None  // Exhausted!
// iter.next() returns None  // Still None
```

### A Simple Iterator Implementation
```xiom
pub type Range = {
  current: Int;
  end: Int;
}

impl Iterator for Range {
  type Item = Int;

  fn next(self: &mut Range) -> Option[Int] {
    if self.current >= self.end { return None(); }
    let value = self.current;
    self.current = self.current + 1;
    return Some(value);
  }

  fn size_hint(self: &Range) -> (Int, Option[Int]) {
    let remaining = self.end - self.current;
    if remaining < 0 { return (0, Some(0)); }
    return (remaining, Some(remaining));
  }
}
```

### Using Iterators
```xiom
let mut range = Range{ current: 0, end: 5 };
while let Some(n) = range.next() {
  io.println(n.to_str());  // 0, 1, 2, 3, 4
}
```

